            <ArticleTitle>Angiogenic factors: potential to change clinical practice in pre-eclampsia?</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/1471-0528.15042</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Pre-eclampsia is a complex disease with significant maternal and fetal morbidity and mortality. Its syndromic nature makes diagnosis and management difficult. The field is rapidly evolving with the definition of pre-eclampsia being challenged by some organisations, with proteinuria no longer being essential in the presence of other features. In the last decade, angiogenic factors, in particular soluble fms-like tyrosine kinase 1 (sFlt-1), have emerged as important molecules in the pathogenesis of pre-eclampsia. Here we review the most recent evidence regarding the potential of these factors as biomarkers and therapeutic targets for pre-eclampsia.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="TWEETABLE ABSTRACT" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">A review of angiogenic factors, sFlt-1 and PlGF, in the diagnosis, prediction and management of pre-eclampsia.</AbstractText>
